course: dictionary
lesson: 1102
title: Bramble: a thorny word from nature
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A bramble is a wild bush with long, arching stems covered in sharp thorns. The blackberry bush is a kind of bramble. Read this: «Move carefully near thorny bushes such as roses and brambles, and get a grown-up's help to pull out a thorn.» The word thorny and the warning to be careful give you clues that brambles prick and can hurt.
explain: Context can show you which meaning is being used. In «caught in the brambles», the word caught tells you something has become stuck in the thorny bushes. In stories, brambles block paths, like the thorny hedge around Sleeping Beauty's castle.
explain: To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence. Does "a prickly wild bush" still make sense in «a bramble bush» or «bramble jelly»? If it does, you have found the right meaning.
fact: A bramble is a wild thorny bush with long arching stems.
fact: Brambles are a thick tangle of prickly bushes, like a wall of thorns.
fact: The blackberry bush is a kind of bramble.
fact: In Scotland and parts of the UK, a bramble also means the blackberry fruit.
fact: Brambles grow in hedges, woods and wasteland, and their flowers feed bees.
exercise: «Move carefully near thorny bushes such as roses and brambles.» What is a bramble? | A soft, smooth flower | *A wild thorny bush | A small pond | A kind of bird
exercise: Phrase: «caught in the brambles». What does it mean? | Happy and free in the sun | *Stuck in a tangle of thorny bushes | Lost in a crowd of people | Eating a big bowl of fruit
exercise: «Stick insects rock like twigs in the wind so hunters ignore them, they eat leaves such as bramble or privet.» What do stick insects eat? | Fish from the pond | Sugar and cake | *Leaves, such as bramble or privet | Mice and worms
test: In a hedge, what do bramble flowers feed? | Fish | Stick insects | *Bees | Caravans
test: In Scotland, what else can bramble mean? | *The blackberry fruit | A pot of stew | A stick insect | A thorny castle wall
exam: Which phrase shows that a bramble can hurt you? | «An iron pot of stew on a fire» | «Bramble jelly» with no warning given | «Stick insects rock like twigs» | *«Move carefully near thorny bushes»
